Here's the complete migration guide for your ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) code.

## Pattern Inventory

Your code has two `Thread.Abort`-related patterns:

| Location | API | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|---|
| `btnExport_Click` | `Response.End()` | ASP.NET request termination | Return `FileContentResult` |
| `btnRedirect_Click` | `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | ASP.NET request termination | Return `RedirectResult` |
| `btnRedirect_Click` | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow via exception | Remove entirely |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // In ASP.NET Core, return a result object instead of writing
        // to Response directly. The framework handles the response.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            // No second parameter — just return a redirect result.
            // The framework short-circuits; no Thread.Abort needed.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No catch (ThreadAbortException) — it doesn't exist in .NET 8.
        // OperationCanceledException is the modern equivalent if needed.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

### 1. `Response.End()` → return `File(...)`

ASP.NET Framework's `Response.End()` called `Thread.Abort` internally to stop pipeline execution. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. The fix: return an `IActionResult` — the framework writes the response for you and execution naturally ends.

### 2. `Response.Redirect(url, true)` → return `Redirect(url)`

The `true` (endResponse) parameter triggered `Thread.Abort` to halt execution after the redirect header was set. In ASP.NET Core, `return Redirect(url)` both sets the header and ends your method — no abort needed.

### 3. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → removed

This catch block only existed to swallow the exception that `Response.Redirect(url, true)` threw. Since `Redirect()` returns a result object, no exception is thrown. Remove it entirely.

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Using `HttpResponse.Redirect()` instead of returning `Redirect()`** | Calling `Response.Redirect()` directly in ASP.NET Core doesn't stop method execution — code after it still runs. Always use `return Redirect()`. |
| **Writing to `Response.Body` then returning a result** | You can't do both. Pick one: either write directly to `Response.Body` (and return an `EmptyResult`), or return a `FileContentResult`. |
| **Missing `[Authorize]` attribute** | Your old code checked `User.Identity.IsAuthenticated` manually. In ASP.NET Core, prefer the `[Authorize]` attribute — it handles the redirect to login automatically. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | If you later add timeout-based cancellation for `ProcessRequest()`, always wrap `CancellationTokenSource` in `using`. |

## Bonus: Using `[Authorize]` (recommended)

The manual auth check can be replaced entirely:

```csharp
[Authorize] // Framework redirects to login automatically
public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }
}
```

## If `ProcessRequest()` is long-running

Wire up `HttpContext.RequestAborted` so the work stops if the client disconnects:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Redirect(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted
    // to this parameter
    try
    {
        await ProcessRequestAsync(cancellationToken);
        return Redirect("/success");
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        // Client disconnected — let it propagate or return 499
        return StatusCode(499);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        LogError(ex);
        return Redirect("/error");
    }
}
```

**Summary:** The core paradigm shift is from *"write to response, then abort the thread"* to *"return a result object and let the framework handle the response."* No `Thread.Abort`, no `ThreadAbortException`, no `Response.End()`.
